SELECT * FROM USERS 
WHERE REGEXP_LIKE(MAIL,'^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_.\-]*@leetcode\.com$');


The given expression is a SQL query with a REGEXP_LIKE function. Let's break it down step by step:

1. Function Overview:
REGEXP_LIKE is used to check whether a string matches a given regular expression pattern.
The syntax is:
sql
Copy code
REGEXP_LIKE(column_name, 'pattern')
In this case, it checks if the value in the MAIL column matches the specified pattern.
2. Pattern Explanation:
plaintext
Copy code
^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_.\-]*@leetcode\.com$
Let's dissect the pattern:

^:
This asserts the start of the string. It ensures the pattern matches from the very beginning of the MAIL string.

[A-Za-z]:
Matches any single alphabetic character (uppercase or lowercase).

This ensures the email begins with a letter.
[A-Za-z0-9_.\-]*:

Matches zero or more occurrences (*) of:
Letters (A-Za-z),
Digits (0-9),
Underscore (_),
Dot (.),
Hyphen (-).
This allows for valid characters in the username portion of the email before the @.
@leetcode:
Matches the literal string @leetcode.

This ensures the domain part of the email starts with leetcode.
\.:
Matches a literal dot (.).

Since . is a special character in regex, it must be escaped with a backslash (\) to be interpreted as a literal dot.
com:
Matches the literal string com.

This ensures the domain extension is .com.
$:
Asserts the end of the string.

Ensures there are no characters after .com.
3. Full Pattern in Action:
The pattern ensures that the email:
Starts with a letter.
Has valid characters (letters, numbers, _, ., or -) in the username part.
Contains @leetcode.com as the domain.
4. Example Matches:
✅ john_doe@leetcode.com
✅ Jane123@leetcode.com
❌ 123_jane@leetcode.com (starts with a number)
❌ john@leet-code.com (invalid domain)
❌ jane@leetcode.org (wrong extension)
5. Usage in SQL:
This query can be used to filter rows where the MAIL column contains valid emails matching this pattern:

